<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Find the range and quartiles using the data in a given stem and leaf diagram</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<p></p>

<p>The following data organized in a stem and leaf diagram is for ages of people who patronized a certain gambling facility in town</p>
<p>
<head>
    <title>Stem and Leaf Diagram</title>
    <style>
        .stem-and-leaf {
            display: inline-block;
            font-family: monospace;
            font-size: 16px;
            line-height: 1.5;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>
    <h1>Stem and Leaf Diagram</h1>
    <div class="stem-and-leaf">
        <pre>
         {#s_1#} | {#l_1#}  {#l_2#}  {#l_3#}
         {#s_2#} | {#l_3+1#}   {#l_4+2#}   {#l_5+3#}
         {#s_3#} |  {#l_3#}   {#l_4#}  {#l_5#}  {#l_3#}  {#l_4#}  {#l_5#}
         {#s_4#} | {#l_1+1#}  {#l_3+2#}  {#l_3+3#}  {#l_1+5#}   {#l_5#} 
         {#s_5#} | {#l_2#}  {#l_3#}  {#l_6#}   {#l_5#}   {#l_6#}
         {#s_6#} | {#l_3#}   {#l_1#}
         {#s_7#} | {#l_2#}
         {#s_8#} | {#l_6#}
        </pre>
    </div>
</body>
</p>
<p>Find the range</p>
<p>[[input:ans1]] [[validation:ans1]][[feedback:prt1]]</p>
<p>Obtain the first second and third quartiles.</p>
<p>\(Q_1=\)  [[input:ans2]] [[validation:ans2]][[feedback:prt2]]</p>
<p>\(Q_2=\)  [[input:ans3]] [[validation:ans3]][[feedback:prt3]]</p>
<p>\(Q_3=\)  [[input:ans4]] [[validation:ans4]][[feedback:prt4]]</p>
<p></p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="moodle_auto_format">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>To find the range in a stem and Leaf diagram, you first Identify the Minimum and Maximum Values:</p>
<p>The minimum value is the smallest data point in your dataset. You can find it by looking at the smallest leaf in the plot.</p>
<p>The maximum value is the largest data point in your dataset. You can find it by looking at the largest leaf in the plot.</p>
<p>The range of a dataset is defined as the difference between the maximum and minimum values. It is a measure of how spread out or varied the data is.</p>
<p>Range = Maximum Value - Minimum Value</p>
<p>From our diagram the Maximum value is \({@max_no@}\) and the minimum value is \({@min_no@}\)</p>
<p>So, the range is \({@ta1@}\)</p>
<p>To find the Quartiles, we first find the median</p>
<p>Using the formula \(Q_2=\frac{1}{2}(n+1)^{th} \text{term}\) where \(n\) is the total number of values that we have.</p>
<p>For this case our \(n=14^{th} \text{term}\)</p>
<p>The median is the \(14^{th}\) number which is \({@ta2@}\)</p>
<p>To find the first quartile, using the same formulae \(Q_2=\frac{1}{2}(n+1)^{th} \text{term}\) upto to the \(14^{th} \) value </p>
<p>Our first quartile will be the \(7^{th}\) upto the median which is \({@ta1@}\)</p>
<p>Applying the same technique, the third quartile will be \({@ta3@}\)</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
